1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,900
Good morning, everyone.

2
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,379
In the last few lectures I've been dealing with business finance,

3
00:00:05,379 --> 00:00:09,450
but now I'm going to move on to business systems.

4
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:13,980
And in today's lecture I'm going to talk about what can go wrong

5
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:17,900
when businesses try to copy their own best practices.

6
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:24,105
Once a business has successfully introduced a new process - managing a branch bank,

7
00:00:24,105 --> 00:00:31,264
say, or selling a new product - the parent organisation naturally wants to repeat that success,

8
00:00:31,264 --> 00:00:35,560
and capture it if possible on a bigger scale.

9
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:43,360
The goal, then, is to utilise existing knowledge and not to generate new knowledge.

10
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:48,640
It's a less glamorous activity than pure innovation

11
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:51,420
but it actually happens more often

12
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:52,520
as a matter of fact.

13
00:00:52,520 --> 00:01:01,110
However, surprisingly, getting things right the Second time is not necessarily any simpler than it was the first time.

14
00:01:01,110 --> 00:01:07,788
Now, there's been a lot of research into how companies can repeat their previous successes,

15
00:01:07,788 --> 00:01:12,240
and it certainly hasn't been confined to the United States.

16
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:18,606
It seems that most large industries are trying to repeat their own successes,

17
00:01:18,606 --> 00:01:21,544
and manage the knowledge they've acquired -

18
00:01:21,544 --> 00:01:28,400
but even so it has been shown that the overwhelming majority of attempts fail.

19
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:35,086
A host of studies confirm this, covering a wide range of business settings:

20
00:01:35,086 --> 00:01:38,940
branch banks, retail stores, real estate agencies

21
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,800
factories, call centres, to name but a few.

22
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:49,680
So why do so few managers get things right the second or third time?

23
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,383
Let's consider one reason for failure -

24
00:01:52,383 --> 00:01:56,080
placing too much trust in the people

25
00:01:56,080 --> 00:02:00,490
who are running the successful operation, the 'experts' shall we say.

26
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:06,959
Managers who want to apply existing knowledge typically start off by going to an expert -

27
00:02:06,959 --> 00:02:10,000
such as the person who designed

28
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,290
and is running a successful department store - and picking their brains.

29
00:02:14,290 --> 00:02:17,140
Now, this approach can be used

30
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:22,875
if you want to gain a rough understanding of a particular system,

31
00:02:22,875 --> 00:02:25,260
or understand smaller, isolated problems.

32
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:31,180
The trouble is, even the expert doesn't fully grasp the whole thing

33
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:33,753
because when it comes to complex systems,

34
00:02:33,753 --> 00:02:38,670
the individual components of the process are interwoven with one another.

35
00:02:38,670 --> 00:02:43,680
The expert never has complete access to the necessary information.

36
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:53,200
And the situation's complicated even further by the fact that experts are usually not aware of their own ignorance.

37
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:57,000
The ignorance can take various forms

38
00:02:57,730 --> 00:03:02,910
For instance, a lot of details of the system are invisible to managers.

39
00:03:02,910 --> 00:03:05,514
Some may be difficult to describe -

40
00:03:05,514 --> 00:03:10,520
learned on the job and well known by workers perhaps

41
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,760
but impossible to describe in a way that's helpful.

42
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:20,490
And there are some things that people know or do that they're not even aware of.

43
00:03:20,490 --> 00:03:30,635
Now, let's consider two types of mistake that can occur

44
00:03:30,715 --> 00:03:36,730
when a manager actually starts to set up a duplicate system to replicate a successful process

45
00:03:36,730 --> 00:03:43,360
Firstly, perhaps he forgets that he was just trying to copy another process,

46
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:46,930
and starts trying to improve on it.

47
00:03:46,930 --> 00:03:53,021
Another mistake is trying to use the best parts of various different systems,

48
00:03:53,021 --> 00:03:56,770
in the hope of creating the perfect combination.

49
00:03:56,770 --> 00:04:05,500
Unfortunately, attempts like these usually turn out to be misguided and lead to problems.

50
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:08,556
Why? Well, for various reasons.

51
00:04:08,556 --> 00:04:13,840
Perhaps there weren't really any advantages after all

52
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:16,500
because the information wasn't accurate.

53
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:21,090
Or perhaps the business settings weren't really comparable.

54
00:04:21,090 --> 00:04:26,780
More typically, the advantages are real enough

55
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:30,890
but there are also disadvantages that have been overlooked.

56
00:04:30,890 --> 00:04:37,280
For example, the modifications might compromise safety in some way.

57
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:39,898
So, what's the solution? Well,

58
00:04:39,898 --> 00:04:48,800
I don't intend to suggest that it's easy to get things right the second time, it's not.

59
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:55,947
But the underlying problem has more to do with attitudes than the actual difficulty of the task,

60
00:04:55,947 --> 00:04:59,310
and there are ways of getting it right.

61
00:04:59,310 --> 00:05:05,500
These involve adjusting attitudes, first of all

62
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:09,100
being more realistic and cautious really.

63
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:18,000
Secondly, they involve exerting strict controls on the organisational and operational systems.

64
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,060
And this in turn means copying the original as closely as possible.

65
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:29,275
Not merely duplicating the physical characteristics of the factory,

66
00:05:29,275 --> 00:05:35,070
but also duplicating the skills that the original employees had.

67
00:05:35,070 --> 00:05:44,110
Reliance on a template like this offers the huge advantage of built-in consistency.
